# Scenario S2: no run replication (SYNTHETIC)

Generated by `scenarios/run.py S2` from `writeup.md.in`; every number below is filled from
`output/results.json` or `output/comparison.json`. The data are synthetic and deliberately
constructed (DECISIONS.md D-086): they show how Stratum behaves, not how a real process
varies.

## What the experiment appears to ask

Does the new anneal change the response compared with the baseline anneal? Each anneal was
given to one run, with six wafers per run and nine sites per wafer:
{{descriptive.measurements_per_level.new}} measurements per anneal. The raw averages are
{{descriptive.means.baseline}} (baseline) and {{descriptive.means.new}} (new), a raw
difference of {{descriptive.raw_mean_difference}}. That number is descriptive only.

## What the design actually supports

No test of the anneal. The anneal was applied to whole runs, so runs are the units that
received it, and there is one run per anneal. Two runs always differ somewhat, for reasons
that have nothing to do with the anneal, and with one run per anneal there is no way to see
how large that run-to-run difference is. Stratum therefore refuses the declaration
(`replication`, condition T2.replicated) before any analysis. The 108 measurements do not
become replicates of the anneal: every wafer and site of a run shares that run's conditions.

## What the allocation analysis shows

Stratum still reads the data to plan a better experiment (planning quantities, not results;
DECISIONS.md D-081). The wafer-to-wafer and site-to-site variation can be measured:
{{results.allocation.components.component=sub1.estimate}} and
{{results.allocation.components.component=sub2.estimate}}. The run-to-run component cannot
be measured at all ({{results.allocation.unit_stratum_df}} degrees of freedom). So every
standard error is only a lower bound:

- the current design: at least {{results.allocation.comparisons.0.scenarios.id=current.se_lower}};
- one more wafer per run: at least {{results.allocation.comparisons.0.scenarios.id=add_wafer.se_lower}};
- one more site per wafer: at least {{results.allocation.comparisons.0.scenarios.id=add_site.se_lower}};
- one more run per anneal: at least {{results.allocation.comparisons.0.scenarios.id=add_unit.se_lower}},
  and with two runs per anneal the run-to-run variation becomes measurable at all (the error
  degrees of freedom go from {{results.allocation.comparisons.0.scenarios.id=current.error_df}}
  to {{results.allocation.comparisons.0.scenarios.id=add_unit.error_df}}).

Wafers and sites only shrink the part of the uncertainty that comes from wafers and sites.
The run-to-run part, which the floor 2 sigma2[run] / r describes, is untouched by them and
is not even estimable here. Stratum does not print a ranking of the options, because with
lower bounds alone the order is not determined by the data. What it states is the floor:
"{{results.allocation.comparisons.0.floor_statement}}"

## What to conclude

Nothing about the anneal yet, however many wafers and sites were measured. The remedy is
more runs per anneal; measuring the existing runs more intensively cannot substitute for
them.

Preregistered expectation: {{comparison.matched}} of {{comparison.assertions}} assertions
match (status {{comparison.status}}).
